From: Andrew Morton <akpm@linux-foundation.org>
Subject: mm: prevent pre-content bypass through mremap and remap_file_pages
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 18:23:13 -0800

A pre-content handler is notified before mmap() exposes a file range, but
mremap() can grow a file VMA beyond its existing range without notifying
the handler. remap_file_pages() can replace a mapping with a different
file range without notification. A caller with ordinary file access can
therefore see data before the handler fills it, or without giving it a
chance to deny that range.

Reject mremap() growth past the current VMA end when the file mode allows
pre-content checks. Resizing within the existing VMA still works because
mmap() already covered that range. This deliberately makes some previously
successful mremap() calls fail with EOPNOTSUPP, even if a handler would
allow the access; the caller can use mmap() to request the new file range.
The file mode can also be set by a legacy FAN_ACCESS_PERM listener without
a pre-content mark, so such files may also be refused. Calling the
potentially blocking handler while holding mmap_write_lock would risk
deadlock, so do not add that call here.

remap_file_pages() already drops mmap_lock around security_mmap_file().
Call the pre-content hook there too, for the target file range.

An unprivileged process can reach the mremap() case with read access to a
watched file. remap_file_pages() also requires a writable shared mapping.
Neither path bypasses ordinary file-opening permissions. The observable
failure depends on a handler that fills content or denies specific ranges.

Fixes: 066e053fe208 ("fsnotify: add pre-content hooks on mmap()")
Signed-off-by: Andrew Morton <akpm@linux-foundation.org>
Reported-by: n3rd <18670333770@163.com>
Closes: https://lore.kernel.org/r/4f296d67.bbe3e.1a1c54655f8.Coremail.18670333770@163.com
